Scenario 1: fm_idle_fleet_capacity refuses (exit 4) when config/fleet-capacity is absent
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ fm_idle_fleet_capacity <throwaway-config-dir-with-no-fleet-capacity-file>
STDOUT=[] STATUS=4

Scenario 2 & 3 (part A - pure vocabulary + condition layer): tests/fm-idle-fleet.test.sh
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ran the repo's real test file (real bin/fm-watch.sh subprocess + real
bin/fm-task-kind-lib.sh + bin/fm-idle-fleet-lib.sh; no fixtures replacing the
scripts under test) via `mutex bin/fm-test-run.sh tests/fm-idle-fleet.test.sh`:

ok - an unstated capacity is refused and reported, never guessed at
ok - a configured capacity is read and every malformed form is refused
ok - in-progress counts open work only, never concluded agents or secondmates
ok - the kinds a spawn records are pinned, and each one is deliberately classified
ok - every kind a spawn records is counted, or deliberately not, with none silently skipped
ok - two workers under way are counted as two, against the capacity the home actually stated
ok - the condition needs free capacity AND ready work, and reports its numbers
ok - an unstated capacity, a malformed one, and an unreadable queue are three separate refusals
ok - a sustained idle fleet with ready work raises one durable alarm
ok - a fleet at its cap, or with nothing to start, raises nothing
ok - the sustain window is served exactly, and clearing the condition retires the episode
ok - a condition that keeps holding re-surfaces on its own cadence, not on every scan
ok - an alarm still queued is not duplicated by a later scan
ok - the scan cadence bounds how often the backlog is read at all
ok - a capacity the detector cannot read is reported once and retires its episode
ok - a capacity that was never stated disables the detector loudly, and repairing it restores the alarm
ok - an unreadable ready queue is logged, and neither re-reported nor allowed to reset the window
ok - away mode escalates the idle-fleet alarm instead of self-handling it
ok - away mode escalates a detector it can no longer run
FM_TEST_END ... exit=0 duration_ms=21917 gate_skip=false

"a capacity that was never stated disables the detector loudly, and repairing it
restores the alarm" is idle_fleet_tick driven as a real bin/fm-watch.sh
subprocess against a throwaway home with state/, config/ (no fleet-capacity),
a task record, and a ready backlog count; it asserts the emitted
"check: fleet idle detector disabled: <config>/fleet-capacity is absent, so
this home has not said how many tasks it runs at once" wake line.

Scenario 3 (part B - fm-spawn.sh --relaunch refusal, live subprocess): ad hoc driver
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Built a throwaway case dir (tests/lib.sh's fm_git_worktree + a stub tmux
identical in shape to tests/fm-control-relaunch.test.sh's make_tmux_stub) with
a real task record whose kind=bogus-kind (a value outside
fm_task_kinds' vocabulary), then invoked the real bin/fm-spawn.sh binary
(not a fixture) with --relaunch --harness claude. The command passed through
every earlier live gate (worktree check, endpoint validation, dead-agent
state check via the tmux stub) before reaching the kind check:

EXIT=1
OUTPUT:
error: task rlx1's record has kind 'bogus-kind', which is not one this firstmate
records (ship scout secondmate); refusing to relaunch a record no reader can
classify
---
keys-sent-bytes=0   (confirms nothing was sent to the pane / no agent launched)
